# 0110: off-by-default Q4_K wave64 decode mat-vec V3 and value-selected variants
#
# Ticket .scratch/beta7-unified-performance/issues/07 step 2b. STUDY.md ranks the two top causes
# of the 44-52 GB/s decode read rate as (1) the block header being reloaded once per row and
# duplicated across every lane covering the block, and (2) 4-8 byte quant loads instead of the
# 16-byte loads that reach 204-206 GB/s in the read4 probe. XEON_MV_Q4K_V3 picks a new kernel:
#   1 = _L8r16_v3: 8 lanes/block, 16 B quant load/lane, one 16 B header load broadcast by 4 simd_shuffle; nr0=16
#   2 = _L8r16_v3ns: same load shape, all 8 lanes load the same 16 B header, no shuffle; nr0=16
#   3 = _L8r8_v3ns: same as 2 with nr0=8, so twice the threadgroups and fewer sumf registers
#   4 = _L16r16_v3u2: 16 lanes/block, 8 B quant load/lane, every lane loads the header, nr0=16
#
# Load shape (per lane, per block, per row), next to the shipped kernels:
#                        bytes/load  lanes/block  quant loads  header req/wave/row  act loads/lane/block
#   _L16r8 (shipped)         4           16            2             64 x 16 B            16 x 4 B
#   _r8 (shipped)            8            8            2             64 x 16 B            32 x 4 B
#   _L16r16_v2 (0109)        4           16            2             64 x 16 B            16 x 4 B / 16 rows
#   _L8r16_v3   (=1)        16            8            1              8 x 16 B + shuffle  8 x 16 B / 16 rows
#   _L8r16_v3ns (=2)        16            8            1              8 x 16 B            8 x 16 B / 16 rows
#   _L8r8_v3ns  (=3)        16            8            1              8 x 16 B            8 x 16 B / 8 rows
#   _L16r16_v3u2(=4)         8           16            1             16 x 16 B            4 x 16 B / 16 rows
#   Consecutive lanes read consecutive quant bytes; the runs sit 144 B apart, as the q4_K block
#   layout requires. Unpacking uses the shipped Q4_K masks and scale folds, so the algebra is unchanged.
#
# Reduction order changed. Values 1-3 keep one low and one high 16-activation group per lane.
# Value 4 keeps one low and one high 8-activation group per lane; the 64-lane simd_sum adds the
# partial sums in a different order than _L16r8/v2, so last-bit differences are possible and
# eval-mode tolerance decides. A CPU-only simulation of the value 4 mapping matched dequantize_row_q4_K
# to 6e-14; no GPU program was run.
#
# Off by default; unset or any other value leaves the default path byte-for-byte unchanged. The
# hook runs before V2, so every value wins when both switches are set. Selection needs Q4_K, n=1,
# simd_width==64, wave64 decode, not the small path, rows a multiple of 16, ne00 a multiple of
# QK_K, 16 B aligned src0/src1 row strides and data pointers. nr0/nsg are set in the picker and
# carried by the kernel name, never overridden afterwards. Threadgroup memory stays 0 B and threads stay 64.
# Adds one odd-nb q4_K eval shape (m=2560, n=1, k=2304) to test-backend-ops; 0109 already added the
# three k=2560 decode shapes. Checked with xcrun -sdk macosx metal -c; no engine build was run.
#
# Measured round 1 by orchestrator (engine v0.2.1-0110-avx1, card 1, test-backend-ops perf, n=1):
#   eval 1836/1836 with and without XEON_MV_Q4K_V3=1; 4B and 8B 256-token greedy text byte-identical;
#   kernel_mul_mv_q4_K_f32_L8r16_v3_w64 loaded when the switch is set;
#   us/run default -> v3: m=9728 k=2560 296.6 -> 546.1; m=4096 k=2560 134.8 -> 226.4;
#   m=2560 k=2560 113.1 -> 152.5; m=14336 k=2560 423.7 -> 518.8; m=4096 k=4096 174.3 -> 182.8;
#   m=12288 k=4096 457.4 -> 506.1; m=151936 k=4096 4904 -> 5792;
#   4B decode (-p 0 -n 128 -r 5 -fa 1, six alternating processes): default 16.642-16.648, v3 13.796-13.815 tok/s.
#   v3 is slower everywhere, worst at k=2560. Values 2-4 not yet measured.
# Measured round 2 by orchestrator (engine v0.2.1-0110b-avx1 = pin d81235049 + series through 0110, card 1 only,
#   GGML_METAL_DEVICE_INDEX=1; card 0 not run, needs the user's approval):
# - eval test-backend-ops -o MUL_MAT: 1836/1836, 0 FAIL for default and values 1-4.
# - perf us/run q4_K n=1 (one process each), default / v1 / v2 / v3 / v4:
#   m=9728 k=2560 296.7 / 386.6 / 278.6 / 271.3 / 252.6;  m=4096 k=2560 134.7 / 154.1 / 116.1 / 122.4 / 122.1
#   m=4096 k=4096 174.2 / 182.7 / 134.8 / 144.2 / 167.2;  m=8192 k=8192 659.6 / 658.1 / 493.2 / 524.9 / 633.1
#   m=151936 k=4096 4897 / 5793 / 4364 / 4173 / 4121;    m=2560 k=2560 113.0 / 152.6 / 113.7 / 110.1 / 122.0
# - decode llama-bench -ngl 99 -p 0 -n 128 -r 5 -fa 1, Qwen3 Q4_K_M, alternating processes, warmup discarded by llama-bench:
#   4B (6 rounds): default 16.644-16.647, v2 16.881-16.904, v3 17.015-17.034, v4 16.941-16.978 t/s
#   8B (4 rounds): default 10.223-10.225, v2 12.226-12.229, v3 11.802-11.806, v4 10.996-10.998 t/s
# - 256-token greedy text via llama-server: 4B 91b358aa and 8B 3757ac38 for every arm (byte-identical).
# - Positive control: llama-server -v logs show _L8r16_v3ns / _L8r8_v3ns / _L16r16_v3u2 loaded for values 2/3/4,
#   _L16r8_w64 + _r8_w64 for the default.
# - Value 1 vs 2 differ only in header broadcast (simd_shuffle) vs per-lane header load; 2 is faster on every
#   shape: consistent with simd_shuffle being costly on GCN 1.0 Metal, not proven (no ISA read).
